---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import B from "../data/buckets.json";

const FAMLAB = { blazevic: "Blažević", prpic: "Prpić", papic: "Papić",
  zubrinic: "Žubrinić", perpic: "Perpić", kalanj: "Kalanj", boras: "Boras",
  sestan: "Šestan", vukelic: "Vukelić" };
const pctAll = Math.round(100 * B.connected / B.publishedTotal);
const isl = B.islands || [];
const home = isl.find((i) => i.home) || isl[0];
const rest = isl.filter((i) => !i.home);
const top8 = rest.slice(0, 8).reduce((a, i) => a + i.n, 0);
// Perpic has no page of its own — it folds into Prpic, as everywhere else here.
const PAGE = { perpic: "prpic" };
const famHref = (k) => "/" + (PAGE[k] || k);

const FAMLABEL = { blazevic: "Blažević", prpic: "Prpić", papic: "Papić",
                   zubrinic: "Žubrinić", perpic: "Perpić", kalanj: "Kalanj" };
const byFam = Object.entries(
  B.orphaned.reduce((a, o) => ((a[o.family] = (a[o.family] || 0) + 1), a), {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1]);
const spec = B.labels.filter((l) => l.specific);
const vague = B.labels.filter((l) => !l.specific);
const pctVague = Math.round(100 * B.vague.agree / (B.vague.agree + B.vague.disagree));
---
<Base title="Labels Where the Father Should Be"
  eyebrow={`${B.buckets.length} sorting buckets, ${B.hanging} published people`}
  dek="This archive found four of these being published as ancestors and removed them, because publishing research scaffolding would be a lie. They were removed from the list of people. Nobody removed them from the list of parents — and it turns out two-thirds of the archive is standing on them.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">A <strong>sorting bucket</strong> is an entry like
    <span class="mono">Unknown Sorting Working Blažević</span> or
    <span class="mono">Brothers [of Josephus] Žubrinić</span>. No priest wrote those words and
    nobody was ever called them. They are labels somebody typed into the father's slot while
    working through a pile of records — and they are still there, still doing the job of a
    parent.</p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>The scale of it</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>
          <tr><th>Buckets acting as a parent</th><td><strong class="mono">{B.buckets.length}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th>Published people hanging off one</th><td><strong class="mono">{B.hanging}</strong></td></tr>
          <tr><th>With <em>no other parent at all</em></th><td><strong class="mono">{B.orphaned.length}</strong></td></tr>
        </tbody></table>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Whose loose ends they are</h3>
        <table class="kv"><tbody>{byFam.map(([f, n]) => (
          <tr><th>{FAMLABEL[f] || f}</th><td><strong class="mono">{n}</strong></td></tr>))}</tbody></table>
        <p class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Only the Žubrinići have ever had their loose ends
        counted. <a href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>That page</a> was itself wrong by 25 until today,
        for exactly this reason.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The biggest ones</h2>
    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>The label</th><th>Family</th><th>Published children</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{B.buckets.slice(0, 12).map((b) => (
        <tr><td class="mono" style="font-size:.88em">{b.name}</td>
          <td class="dim">{FAMLABEL[b.family] || b.family}</td>
          <td class="mono">{b.published}</td></tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Do the labels encode anything real?</h2>
    <p class="lede">Somebody grouped these children for a reason. <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136
    For Sorting Perpić</span> carries a house number; <span class="mono">1800-1830 Birth — Selce
    Papić (Papa)</span> carries a village, a date range and a
    <a href={u("/name")}>nadimak</a>. The question is whether the reason survives well enough to
    place a person by — and it can be tested, because some of the children under each label have a
    place of their own on record.</p>

    <p><strong>It splits sharply, and on one thing: how specific the label is.</strong></p>

    <div class="grid two" style="margin-top:1.2rem">
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Labels naming a house number or a date range</h3>
        <p style="font-size:1.6em;margin:.2rem 0"><strong class="mono">{B.specific.agree} agree</strong>
          <span class="dim"> / {B.specific.disagree} disagree</span></p>
        <p class="dek">Every testable child matches. <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136</span> is 5 for 5;
        <span class="mono">1800-1830 Selce</span> is 3 for 3 on place <em>and</em> 3 for 3 on the year
        range. These were written by someone who knew something.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <h3>Labels naming only a town</h3>
        <p style="font-size:1.6em;margin:.2rem 0"><strong class="mono">{B.vague.agree} agree</strong>
          <span class="dim"> / {B.vague.disagree} disagree</span></p>
        <p class="dek">Right {pctVague}% of the time — worse than useless, because it looks like
        information. <span class="mono">Senj – Unknown To be sorted Prpic</span> is wrong on every
        one of the four children who can test it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What it claims</th><th>Agree</th><th>Disagree</th><th>Untestable</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{[...spec, ...vague].map((l) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="mono" style="font-size:.86em">{l.name.slice(0, 44)}</td>
          <td class="dim">{[l.village, l.house && "kbr " + l.house,
                            l.range && l.range.join("–")].filter(Boolean).join(" · ")}</td>
          <td class="mono">{l.agree}</td>
          <td class="mono">{l.disagree > 0 ? <strong>{l.disagree}</strong> : l.disagree}</td>
          <td class="mono dim">{l.blank}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>So: {B.specific.blank + B.vague.blank} published people
    with no place of their own sit under a label that names one — and only {B.specific.blank} of
    those sit under a label worth believing.</strong> The rest cannot be placed this way, and this
    archive will not place them.</p>
    <p class="dek">Which is a result worth having either way. The two specific labels are worth
    taking to a registrar as a question; the vague ones should be treated as what they are, a note
    to self by someone half way through a sorting job.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The uncomfortable part</div>
    <h2>Delete the labels and two-thirds of this archive floats free</h2>
    <p class="lede">The section above asks whether a label is <em>accurate</em>. This asks something
    harder. If every sorting label were removed from the tree, how many of the people published here
    would still be connected to <a href={u("/hedviga")}>Hedviga</a> at all — by any path, however
    long, of parents, children and spouses?</p>

    <p><strong>{B.connected} of {B.publishedTotal}. {pctAll}%.</strong></p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Published</th><th>Still connected</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{B.standing.map((x) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={u(famHref(x.family))}>{FAMLAB[x.family] || x.family}</a></td>
          <td class="mono dim">{x.published}</td>
          <td class="mono">{x.connected}</td>
          <td class="mono"><strong>{x.pct}%</strong></td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>Šestan is nought per cent</h3>
      <p>All <strong>55</strong> of them. The shortest path from Hedviga to the nearest Šestan runs
      eleven steps — out through her <em>husband's</em> family, the Defranceski, back through
      Teresa Grach, and then across a node the tree itself calls
      <span class="mono">Grah's for Investigation (not real)</span>. Take that node away and there
      is no path at all.</p>
      <p>The archive describes this family as <em>“of the Croatian Littoral”</em>. Twenty-three of
      the twenty-four placed Šestani are at <strong>Gologorica</strong>, which is in central
      <strong>Istria</strong> — a different peninsula, eighty miles west. And not one Šestan marries
      into any of the other eight families. They are here because somebody was investigating
      whether they belonged, and the investigation is still sitting in the tree as though it were an
      ancestor.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <h3>And the Žubrinići are five per cent</h3>
      <p>Twenty-one of four hundred and fourteen. This is the <em>largest</em> family in the
      archive and the subject of <a href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>its longest-running question</a> —
      and the great majority of them reach Hedviga only by way of a label. Prpić is 6%, Perpić 8%,
      Blažević 23%, Papić 27%.</p>
      <p>Two are whole. <strong>Boras and Kalanj are 100%</strong> — every published member connects
      by ordinary parent, child and marriage links. For Kalanj that chain was
      <a href={u("/grave")}>confirmed from registers this month</a>: Ana Kalanj of Klenovica 22
      married Josip de Franceschi, and their line runs to Hedviga's husband.</p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.4rem"><strong>What this does and does not mean.</strong> It does
    <em>not</em> mean these people are invented, or that the research was careless — the
    <a href={u("/register")}>register</a> is full of real entries about real people, and this
    archive publishes by <em>surname group</em>, never having claimed each person is a proven
    relative. What it means is narrower and still worth saying out loud: for most of the names here,
    <strong>the link to Hedviga is currently made by a sorting note rather than by a document</strong>.
    A reader is entitled to know which two-thirds.</p>
    <p class="dek">It also reorders the work. Reading one Otočac marriage that joins a Žubrinić
    fragment to the line is worth more than reading fifty pages that confirm dates already held.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">The useful way to say it</div>
    <h2>It is not {B.publishedTotal - B.connected} loose people. It is {B.islandCount} islands.</h2>
    <p class="lede">“Two-thirds of the archive reaches Hedviga only through a label” sounds like
    eight hundred separate problems, and it is not. Take the labels away and the published people do
    not scatter — they fall into <strong>{B.islandCount} coherent families</strong> that are simply
    not joined to hers. Most are internally complete: parents, children, marriages, house numbers.
    They are whole households missing one link at the top.</p>
    <p><strong>And the distribution is kind.</strong> Hedviga's own island holds
    <strong>{home?.n}</strong> people. The next eight hold <strong>{top8}</strong> between them. So
    the gap is not eight hundred documents — <strong>eight marriage entries would close most of
    it.</strong></p>

    <table class="reg">
      <thead><tr><th>People</th><th>Span</th><th>Who they are</th><th>The one to look for</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {home && (
          <tr style="background:color-mix(in srgb, var(--accent) 9%, transparent)">
            <td class="mono"><strong>{home.n}</strong></td>
            <td class="mono dim">{home.from}–{home.to}</td>
            <td>{home.families.map(([f, n]) => `${f} ${n}`).join(" · ")}
              {" "}<strong>— Hedviga's own</strong></td>
            <td class="dim">—</td>
          </tr>)}
        {rest.slice(0, 9).map((i) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="mono"><strong>{i.n}</strong></td>
            <td class="mono dim">{i.from}–{i.to}</td>
            <td class="dim">{i.families.map(([f, n]) => `${f} ${n}`).join(" · ")}</td>
            <td>{i.bridge
              ? <span>{i.bridge.slug
                  ? <a href={u("/people/" + i.bridge.slug)}>{i.bridge.name}</a>
                  : i.bridge.name}
                  {" "}<span class="mono dim">b.{i.bridge.byear}</span>
                  {i.bridge.place && <span class="dim"> · {i.bridge.place.split(",")[0]}</span>}
                  {i.bridge.children > 0 && <span class="dim"> · {i.bridge.children} children</span>}
                </span>
              : <span class="dim">nothing dated enough to search</span>}</td>
          </tr>))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="dek" style="margin-top:1rem">The candidate is the <strong>earliest</strong> person in
    each island who still carries a birth year — because the join is missing at the old end, and
    that is where a marriage entry would attach it. Ties go to whoever has most children, since
    joining them moves the most people.</p>

    <div class="qa" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
      <h3>Three of these are worth a morning each</h3>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><strong>The 217-person Žubrinić island.</strong> Larger than most whole families on this
        site, and connected to Hedviga by nothing. The oldest in it is
        <strong>Nicolai Žubrinić, born 1769</strong> — so the marriage would fall about
        <strong>1787–1799</strong> at Otočac.</li>
        <li><strong>Josephus Žubrinić, born 1782 at <span class="mono">Prozor 46</span></strong>,
        with <strong>eight children</strong> — the single most productive join available, and it
        comes with a village <em>and</em> a house number.</li>
        <li><strong>Domianus Perpić, born 1820 at <span class="mono">Krivi Put 136</span></strong> —
        the same house this archive already works on. Thirty-nine Prpići and Perpići hang off him,
        and the house number means the entry can be found without a name index.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">Which reorders the research completely. This archive has spent its
    effort <em>confirming</em> — finding the register entry behind a date already held. That work
    adds nothing to the number above. <strong>One marriage at Otočac would add two hundred and
    seventeen people.</strong> <a href={u("/searched")}>What to ask for →</a></p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What follows</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>Un-publishing is not removing.</strong> A record hidden from the register goes on
      acting as a parent in every tool that asks whether someone has one. That is how
      <a href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>the fragment count</a> was wrong by 25 for months.</li>
      <li><strong>Nothing here has been edited.</strong> The buckets remain in the tree exactly as
      they are. <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></li>
      <li><strong>Sixty-two of the seventy-four are not Žubrinić.</strong> Thirty Blažević,
      twenty-two Prpić, ten Papić and four Perpić have never been counted as loose ends anywhere on
      this site. They are, and now they are listed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1.2rem"><a class="btn" href={u("/zubrinic-roots")}>The Žubrinić fragments →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/duplicates")}>The same person, twice →</a>
      {" "}<a class="btn" href={u("/impossible")}>What cannot be true →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
